.TH CCOL_SELECTABLE_FROM_CHAN 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_selectable_from_chan \- resolve a ccol_channel into a ccol_selectable for the calling thread and requested direction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_selectable ccol_selectable_from_chan(ccol_channel *ch, ccol_select_dir dir);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A ccol_channel has two circular queues below it. This function reads
the ID of the calling thread and the direction that you ask for, and
from them chooses which of those two queues to wrap:

  ccol_select_read; owner <- workers_to_owner_cq  (same as ccol_chan_recv_zc)
                    worker <- owner_to_workers_cq
  ccol_select_write; owner -> owner_to_workers_cq  (same as ccol_chan_send_zc)
                    worker -> workers_to_owner_cq

This function resolves the queue at the call site, so ccol_select()
itself never needs access to the opaque ccol_channel struct. The
function does not store the ccol_channel pointer: the ccol_selectable
that it returns keeps only the ccol_circular_queue pointer that it
resolved and the dir value, and it watches that one queue, whichever
thread later waits on it or runs a callback for it.

ccol_select() and ccol_event_loop only report that the queue is
ready; the caller does the receive or the send itself.

After ccol_select() returns on the thread that built the selectable,
ccol_chan_try_recv_zc() and ccol_chan_try_send_zc() resolve to the
same queue, and so do ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() and
ccol_circq_try_send_zc() on sel.cq.

Inside a ccol_event_loop callback, use ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() and
ccol_circq_try_send_zc() on sel->cq, and never a ccol_chan_* call.
The callback runs on a thread of the loop, which the channel counts as
a worker whichever thread built the selectable, so for a selectable
that the owner built, a ccol_chan_* call there reaches the other
queue. For example, with a selectable that the owner built for
ccol_select_read, which watches workers_to_owner_cq, a
ccol_chan_try_recv_zc() inside on_readable receives from
owner_to_workers_cq: it takes a message that the owner sent to its
workers, and leaves in the queue the message that made the callback
run.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ch   Channel to resolve.  Must not be NULL.
dir  ccol_select_read or ccol_select_write.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A ccol_selectable of type ccol_selectable_circq that wraps the right
ccol_circular_queue for the calling thread and for the direction.

If ch is NULL, the function returns a ccol_selectable_circq with a
NULL queue pointer, for which ccol_select() returns
ccol_invalid_args.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function resolves the direction one time, at the moment of the
call, and does not evaluate it again on each pass of ccol_select().
Build the selectable again when the role of the calling thread changes
between the build and the real ccol_select() call.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
This function reads ch->owner_tid and two pointer fields, which the
library writes one time, when it creates the ccol_channel, and never
changes after that. The function is therefore safe to call from any
thread, at the same time as any other call.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* The owner watches the replies of its workers on a ccol_event_loop. */
static void on_reply(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg,
                     ccol_selectable *sel, void *arg) {
    c_message_t msg;
    /* sel->cq, and not ccol_chan_try_recv_zc(): this runs on a thread of
       the loop, which the channel counts as a worker. */
    while (ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(sel->cq, &msg) == ccol_success)
        handle_reply(arg, &msg);
}

ccol_event_handlers_t h = { .on_readable = on_reply };
ccol_event_reg reg = ccol_event_loop_add(
    loop, ccol_selectable_from_chan(ch, ccol_select_read), h, ctx, NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_select (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_add (3),
.BR ccol_circq_try_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_try_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_chan_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_chan_send_zc (3)
